  "response": "I’ll run the requested scan, inspect the fixture writer, and bound its concurrency while preserving all fixture files.\n\n\nThe scan confirms “Concurrency Stampede” at `test/utils.ts:38–46` (`crime_00020` in your report). The map starts work for every file immediately, so peak open-file usage grows with fixture size. CI can cross its descriptor limit because of different limits, overlapping work, or slower I/O keeping files open longer. I’ll process batches of 10 and check that every file finishes before the CLI starts.\n\n\nFixed [test/utils.ts](/private/var/folders/8_/6tdb3p2s63v_xxb15r46cqwr0000gn/T/crimes-eval-agent-pdRNaZ/test/utils.ts:37) to write files in awaited batches of **10**, preserving every file and propagating errors.\n\n`crimes scan test --format json` confirmed **Concurrency Stampede** (`unbounded_async_fanout`; `crime_00020` in your supplied report). Its evidence quotes:\n\n> “fan-out: Promise.all at line 38”  \n> “collection: Object.keys(files).map(…)”\n\nThe original map eagerly started one async task per file, each creating its directory and writing its contents. Large fixtures increase overlapping open files. Descriptor limits, existing process activity, test parallelism, and I/O timing vary across machines, so CI can hit `EMFILE` while local runs pass.\n\nVerified the edited write block with mocked filesystem operations for 0–1,003 files: peak concurrency stayed at 10, all contents were written, and errors propagated. The rescan reports no unbounded fan-out. Full suite execution was unavailable because dependencies are not installed.",
